Uveitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Deep aching eye pain, Consensual photophobia, Anterior chamber cell and flare

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-047
Reason for Visit: Acute, deep, throbbing eye pain with severe sensitivity to light and blurring of vision.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with sudden onset of severe, deep unilateral eye pain, profound photophobia, and a non-reactive pupil. Urgent face-to-face assessment was absolutely required to definitively differentiate acute anterior uveitis (iritis) from acute angle-closure glaucoma or infectious keratitis. This is a sight-threatening emergency requiring immediate diagnosis and targeted topical steroid therapy to prevent permanent synechiae (adhesions) and irreversible vision loss prior to morning specialist availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My eye has a deep, throbbing ache like a toothache, and turning on the room light feels like a knife stabbing into my brain."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began 12 hours ago and have rapidly worsened. Patient describes a deep, dull, aching eye pain in the right eye that worsens with eye movement and accommodation. Endorses severe photophobia; notably, shining a light in the unaffected left eye causes severe pain in the right eye (consensual photophobia). Reports blurred vision and the sudden appearance of floaters. Denies any superficial gritty or sandy sensation, and explicitly denies any stringy, watery, or mucopurulent conjunctival discharge. Has a known history of ankylosing spondylitis.
Functional Status: Incapacitated by photophobia; sitting in a pitch-black room wearing sunglasses.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high fall risk navigating in the dark.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: In severe distress when exposed to ambient light, keeping the right eye tightly closed.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity in the right eye is reduced to 6/18. Left eye is 6/6. The right eye exhibits a prominent perilimbal conjunctival injection (ciliary flush) with the redness concentrated intensely around the iris rather than the fornices. The right pupil is constricted, poorly reactive, and slightly irregular in shape (miosis with early posterior synechiae). Left pupil is normal. Direct ophthalmoscopy with a focused, bright beam (slit-lamp equivalent) reveals a hazy anterior chamber indicating inflammatory cells and proteinaceous flare. Cornea lacks any superficial epithelial defect or white infiltrate. Intraocular pressure (assessed by digital palpation) is soft and symmetric.
Environmental Observations: All lights extinguished; blinds drawn tightly.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes NSAIDs intermittently for back pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Anterior Uveitis (Iritis) - Status: Acute, Severe
Differentials:

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pupil being constricted (miotic) rather than mid-dilated and fixed, the globe feeling soft to palpation (normal IOP), and the cornea remaining clear rather than distinctly cloudy/edematous.

Infectious Keratitis (Corneal Ulcer) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of a visible white focal stromal infiltrate or epithelial defect, and the absolute lack of purulent discharge.

Viral Conjunctivitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of deep aching pain, profound photophobia, ciliary flush, and a constricted pupil, which are completely absent in superficial conjunctival infections.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a classic, severe "red eye" emergency. The critical clinical imperative was to immediately distinguish intraocular inflammation from elevated intraocular pressure or corneal infection, as the treatments are diametrically opposed. Physical examination definitively localized the inflammation to the anterior uveal tract, confirmed by the constricted pupil, ciliary flush, and pathognomonic consensual photophobia. Because severe uveitis can rapidly form permanent adhesions (synechiae) between the iris and lens overnight—leading to secondary blindness—urgent assessment was mandated to initiate aggressive pupillary dilation and secure immediate ophthalmology review to commence intensive topical steroids.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered one drop of Cyclopentolate 1% in the right eye during the visit to achieve immediate cycloplegia. This paralyzed the ciliary body, instantly relieving the severe aching spasm and breaking early posterior synechiae. Generated an urgent, mandatory referral to an ophthalmologist for high-magnification slit-lamp grading and prescription of Prednisolone Acetate 1% drops.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the profound light sensitivity safely while navigating the home, the patient must wear dark wrap-around sunglasses and a wide-brimmed hat indoors. The immediate physical block of overhead ambient light drastically reduces the stabbing photophobic pain (positive reinforcement), ensuring the patient can safely ambulate and attend their urgent specialist appointment without remaining completely bedbound in the dark.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory Ophthalmology review within 12-24 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the vision suddenly goes completely black, if severe intractable vomiting begins, or if the deep eye pain worsens drastically despite the dilating drop.

